# Line-ending policy. There was none, and on the first Windows CI run three
# tests failed because of it — none of them a logic defect.
#
# Git guesses "text or binary" by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8 KB. A
# hand-built PDF fixture can contain none, so git classified
# `tests/fixtures/scanned_no_text.pdf` as TEXT, a Windows checkout rewrote its
# 43 LF bytes as CRLF, every byte offset in the xref table shifted, and
# pdf-extract reported `Invalid file trailer`. The other real PDF in that
# directory survived only because its compressed streams happen to contain NULs.
#
# Separately, artifacts that Rust generates and then compares BYTE FOR BYTE
# against a checked-in copy cannot tolerate CRLF either: `vocabulary.rs`
# produces `\n` and the Windows checkout held `\r\n`.
#
# This is repo configuration, not a concession to CI — a Windows contributor
# cloning the repo hit exactly the same three failures.

# Normalise text in the repository, and check it out with LF everywhere. Byte
# comparisons against generated content only work if both sides agree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Formats that must never be touched, whatever git's heuristic concludes.
*.pdf   binary
*.gguf  binary
*.docx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
